1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
In this video, we are going to talk about UV processing techniques.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
First, let's talk about what is UV2.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:24,000
UV2 can be simply understood as the picture formed by a 3D model of the surface texture to the 2D square area.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
We can take a look at the model of the earth and the UV picture.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
After the surface of a sphere is flattened into a square, it looks like this.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
This is the UV picture of the sphere.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
If we want the texture of the map to appear on this sphere,

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
then we have to place the map in the corresponding position to the sphere in the UV picture.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
And after this UV picture is wrapped on our 3D sphere model,

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
the texture of the map will appear.

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
When we finish 3D clothing and want to import it into other 3D software,

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
we need to process its UV to ensure that the surface of the fabric is clean.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Then we can import it into other software.

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Next, let's open the UV editor mode through the upper right corner.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Because 3D clothing is made up of 2D flat panels,

16
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
the UV picture of 3D clothing is the 3D version of the 3D clothing.

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
We can manually drag and drop to adjust the position of the panels.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Let's do the arrangement of the panels and the UV picture.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
In the tool bar on the right side, there are also a series of UV tools.

20
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Click on the automatic UV fill.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,000
It will open a window.

22
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Here.

23
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Let's talk about the first concept.

24
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,000
UV area.

25
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
For better compatibility between different software.

26
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
It is generally recommended that the UV picture is placed in the default area 01,

27
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
which is our first line,

28
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
in the first unit grid.

29
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Let's click on the application.

30
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
You can see that the UV of the panel will be displayed in the first unit grid.

31
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Next, let's talk about some options in the UV layout.

32
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
First of all, the first one is fill.

33
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
It controls the gap between each panel UV.

34
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Its data is set from 0.999 to 0.001.

35
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
The larger the data, the larger the gap between the panels.

36
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
For example, I changed it to 0.050 here.

37
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
You can see that there will be obvious gaps between the panels.

38
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Here it is still changed to 0.001.

39
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Next, look at the accuracy.

40
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Here the maximum value is 5 and the minimum value is 1.

41
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
In the default situation, in order to ensure the accuracy of the UV layout,

42
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
and more reasonably use the space of the UV line,

43
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
there is a suitable calculation speed.

44
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Set it to 3.

45
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
But when the number of panels is too large,

46
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
and the layout speed is slow,

47
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
you can reduce some accuracy

48
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
to quickly generate a layout result.

49
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Next, let's look at the following options.

50
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
The first one is the fixed ratio.

51
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
The relative size of the panel is default to be the same as the 2D panel.

52
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
And when we cancel the fixed ratio,

53
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
you can see that some panels will be enlarged or reduced

54
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
for the space utilization rate of the layout.

55
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
This will lead to the final 3D model.

56
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
The clarity of the texture between different panels will be a little different.

57
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
So we usually choose the fixed ratio.

58
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Now we can reset the layout of the UV,

59
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
and then select our fixed ratio.

60
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Next is the rotation.

61
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
After selecting the rotation, click on the application.

62
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
You can see that in order to increase the utilization rate of this space,

63
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
it will rotate the entire panel.

64
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Next is the optimization layout.

65
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,000
The optimization layout will be calculated in a more advanced way.

66
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
To layout our UV,

67
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
it will increase the utilization rate of the space.

68
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:21,000
In other words,

69
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
it will ensure that the UV is in the designated area,

70
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,000
and at the same time,

71
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
the panel will be enlarged as much as possible.

72
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
This will make our final fabric texture as clear as possible.

73
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,000
But on the contrary,

74
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
the calculation time it uses will be longer.

75
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Then let's take a look at the UV quick photo tool.

76
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
It can save this current UV picture directly.

77
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Next is the renovation pattern.

78
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
It can follow the current UV that is not good,

79
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
and its corresponding pattern,

80
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,000
hair line picture,

81
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,000
simulation picture,

82
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:50,000
transparent picture,

83
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,000
and other different material pictures,

84
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
are prepared and saved together.

85
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Next is the UV movement.

86
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
We can select the important moving panel,

87
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
then click UV movement,

88
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
you can adjust the overall position of our panel.

89
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
You can enter its moving distance at the bottom.

90
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Now it is moving a unit.

91
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
I modify it to 0.5 units.

92
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,000
At this time,

93
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
0.5 units are moved.

94
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,000
This tool is generally used to adjust the position of the overall UV picture.

95
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
After we have processed the UV picture of 3D clothing,

96
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
we can export it directly through the file,

97
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
and then export it to other 3D graphics.

98
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
In this process,

99
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
it will also export our UV picture.

100
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,000
For example,

101
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,000
we export OBZ,

102
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,000
after choosing,

103
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
it will open this window.

104
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
We can choose to export the thick one.

105
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,000
If it is thin,

106
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
the thickness of the fabric will not be exported.

107
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
So here we come to choose the thick one,

108
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
and also export the thickness of our fabric.

109
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Next,

110
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
we can select the unified UV coordinates.

111
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
So it will export the UV information of our 3D clothing

112
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
and the UV picture together.

113
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
To make sure its texture can be exported normally,

114
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
let's take a look at the options.

115
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:56,000
First of all,

116
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
the first option is the size of the picture.

117
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
It can control the size of our UV picture.

118
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Generally,

119
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
the larger the size of the image,

120
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
the clearer the picture will be,

121
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
but the size of the corresponding model will be larger.

122
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Then the following is the fill texture.

123
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:11,000
The default is 5 pixels.

124
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:12,000
What's the use of this?

125
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
We can take a look at this example below.

126
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Let's import an OBZ.

127
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:17,000
This OBZ

128
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
does not set the fill pixel.

129
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,000
In other words,

130
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
it does not have the advantage of that UV.

131
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Then we can see that

132
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
it will appear in the place where the line is drawn.

133
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
The texture is not completely covered,

134
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
including the edge of the panel.

135
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,000
There is also this phenomenon.

136
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
This is its UV picture.

137
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Then we can look at the UV picture of its line drawing.

138
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
The panel we export is a thick panel.

139
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
But according to the concept of our UV just now,

140
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
you should be able to understand that

141
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:42,000
this line drawing

142
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
has a thicker UV picture.

143
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:45,000
This way,

144
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
we can ensure that the whole line drawing

145
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:48,000
has a texture texture

146
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
so that it can have a texture package.

147
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Therefore,

148
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:52,000
it needs a fill pixel here.

149
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,000
That is to say,

150
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:54,000
its UV pixel edge

151
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
has to have a certain protrusion.

152
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:57,000
In this way,

153
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,000
we can ensure that

154
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,000
the whole model

155
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,000
has a texture package.

156
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Then below,

157
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
let's import an OBZ.

158
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,000
This OBZ

159
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
is the fill texture of 5 pixels.

160
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Then you can see that

161
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
after the fill texture is performed,

162
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
the line drawing will become normal.

163
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,000
But

164
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,000
we will find that

165
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,000
some black edges

166
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,000
will appear in the pocket

167
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,000
and the waist part.

168
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:17,000
And this black edge

169
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,000
is because

170
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
our UV picture

171
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
is too close.

172
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,000
This is the UV picture

173
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,000
of the second model.

174
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,000
You can see that

175
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
after selecting the fill pixel,

176
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
its pixel edge will appear.

177
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Then our drawstring

178
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
can be covered by the finished texture.

179
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
But it also appears

180
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,000
in a collision

181
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:34,000
with other panels,

182
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:35,000
so there will be

183
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
a partial black edge phenomenon.

184
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Therefore,

185
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,000
for some of these panels,

186
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:40,000
the 3D model

187
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,000
will be thicker.

188
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,000
So when we are doing

189
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,000
UV arrangement,

190
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,000
we should pay attention

191
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,000
to a gap

192
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:46,000
between the panels.

193
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:47,000
For example,

194
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:48,000
if we want to

195
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,000
fill the space

196
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,000
between the panels,

197
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,000
we can use

198
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:52,000
the UV arrangement tool.

199
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Then here,

200
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:54,000
we can input 0.005

201
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:55,000
where we fill the space.

202
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:56,000
In this way,

203
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:57,000
there will be a little more

204
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:58,000
gap between our panels.

205
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,000
We can see that

206
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:00,000
now there is a gap

207
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,000
between the panels.

208
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Obviously,

209
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:03,000
there are more gaps.

210
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Then the gap

211
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:05,000
between our drawstring panels

212
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,000
has also become larger.

213
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,000
At this time,

214
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:08,000
it will count the pixel fill

215
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:09,000
and it will not affect

216
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:10,000
our drawstring panels.

217
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Then

218
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,000
we will

219
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:13,000
make this OBZ

220
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
one-touch.

221
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:15,000
The same choice is for the thick one.

222
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Then

223
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:17,000
the pixel is filled

224
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:18,000
with five pixels.

225
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Click OK.

226
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:20,000
Then

227
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,000
let's open

228
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,000
the OBZ we just made.

229
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Here

230
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:24,000
we can see

231
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,000
that its drawstring

232
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,000
is normally displayed.

233
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Moreover,

234
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,000
there is no

235
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,000
a situation

236
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,000
of covering each other

237
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:31,000
between UVs.

238
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:32,000
This is its UV picture.

239
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:33,000
We can see

240
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:34,000
that

241
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:35,000
there is no

242
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,000
covering

243
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,000
between each UV.

244
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,000
This is the content

245
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:39,000
of our UV processing techniques.

246
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Thank you for watching.

247
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
